<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net6.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="6.0.22" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.Linq.Dynamic.Core" Version="1.6.7" />

    <!-- Not used by this library directly. EF Core 6.0.22 asks for 6.0.1 or later, and 6.0.1 is the
         last version open to CVE-2024-43483 (GHSA-qj66-m88j-hmgj), a denial of service through hash
         flooding, so a host on the EF Core 6 floor resolved a vulnerable version through this
         package and its three companions. Naming the patched version here raises that floor for all
         four; a host on EF Core 8 or later already resolves a newer one and sees no change. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory" Version="6.0.2" />

    <!-- The policy posture binds from IConfiguration, which is why these are here rather than in
         the ASP.NET Core package. The schema builder already lives in this assembly on the grounds
         that a host with no web stack should still get discovery; a worker service that wants its
         caps in a file is the same argument, and putting the binding one package up would leave it
         writing the binder call by hand and losing the unknown-key check that is the point of
         shipping this at all.
         All three ship with the shared framework, and this library already depends on EF Core, so
         the graph is not being kept bare. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="6.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder" Version="6.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="6.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The policy gate is internal on purpose: the sanitizer, the deep clones it relies on, and
         the guard check are implementation details of ApplyPolicy, not API a consumer calls. They
         still need direct tests, and publishing them to get those would commit them as surface
         permanently. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DynamicWhere.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
      <PackageId>DynamicWhere.ex</PackageId>
      <Title>DynamicWhere.ex — JSON-driven queries for EF Core</Title>
      <Description>JSON-driven queries for Entity Framework Core. A powerful, versatile library for dynamically composing complex filter, sort, paginate, group, aggregate, and set-operation (Union / Intersect / Except) expressions — all driven by simple JSON objects from any front-end or API consumer. Three composable shapes (Filter / Segment / Summary), twenty-eight extension methods, nested navigation through references and collections with auto-wrapped .Any() lambdas, heterogeneous Condition.Values with type-safe coercion, and a thread-safe reflection cache with five tuned presets (FIFO / LRU / LFU). Since 3.0 it also carries an opt-in field-level policy layer: attributes and runtime rules decide what each caller may filter, sort, select, group, aggregate and see, with masking, tokenization, forced predicates and a k-anonymity floor. Targets .NET 6+. Free Forever. Full reference, JSON cookbook, and tuning guide at https://doc.dynamicwhere.com.</Description>
      <Summary>Dynamic filters, sort, paginate, group, aggregate, and set operations for EF Core — driven by JSON.</Summary>
